## REASONING

**Decomposition.** The request has two goals: (1) a *gradient-characterization* goal — how peak SWE varies with elevation across the Wenatchee basin — and (2) a *validation* goal — how well modeled peak SWE matches SNOTEL. Both center on one fast state, SWE, which ELM simulates and writes to default history (H2OSNO, QSNOMELT, SNOW).

**Feasibility.** This question is unusually well-matched to the stack's real capabilities, with one honest caveat. The single driver is elevation, and elevation matters here *through orographic precipitation and the temperature control on snow-vs-rain and melt*. The NLDAS-2 default forcing (0.125°, ~12 km) DOES vary precip and temperature with location and elevation across a basin, so an elevation-stratified column set genuinely samples an elevation/climate gradient — this is exactly the case the framework can pull. The caveat is resolution: the 2683 m of relief is only resolved to ~12 km cells, so sub-grid terrain (steep valley/ridge contrasts within a cell) is not captured, and there is no within-cell lapse rate. Consequently ELM is expected to **underestimate peak SWE at high, terrain-exposed SNOTEL stations** because the 12 km cell averages over lower surrounding terrain. This is a known, documented limit — I report it, don't design around it. Verdict: **partial** — the gradient and the SNOTEL comparison are both answerable; the *magnitude* skill at ridgetop stations is limited by forcing resolution, and that limitation is itself a reportable result.

**No deep spin-up needed.** SWE is a seasonal fast state that fully re-accumulates each winter, so the multi-year water-table spin-up concern does not apply. I do want several winters (multi-year forcing period) to build a peak-SWE climatology and to overlap the SNOTEL record, not one anomalous year.

**Drivers & N.** Only elevation is a candidate driver. I stratify into elevation bands spanning 187–2870 m. Validation columns are dictated by data: 5, pinned to the 5 SNOTEL stations (co-located). Exploratory columns fill the gradient between/around stations — I use ~7 columns across ~5 bands with light concentration in the rain–snow transition (roughly 1200–1800 m) where peak-SWE sensitivity to warming/elevation is greatest. Total ≈ 12.

**Placement.** Concentrate exploratory columns in the rain–snow transition band (steepest dSWE/dz) and ensure at least one column low (near 187 m, likely ephemeral/no snow) and one near the crest (~2870 m) to bracket the gradient. SNOTEL columns are fixed to station coordinates (not provided in brief — flagged).

